<p>Oh huh, Agent Green. That's kind of interesting, I guess.</p>
<p>This work is really insubstantial<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-639305-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-639305-1')">1</a></sup>. There's whiffs of something interesting<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-639305-2" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-639305-2')">2</a></sup>, but it feels like its all obscured in favor of a payoff that never comes. The object itself is fairly humdrum; it is a killbot made of nitrogen. Potentially interesting questions, such as who made it, why its made of nitrogen, what purpose it's supposed to serve, etc. are addressed, as far as I can tell, in the article.</p>
<p>The bulk of the article is Agent Green's account of initial containment, which fails to give any meaningful characterization, new information, or even particularly striking images. At the end, I was left waiting for a last-line reveal of &quot;and tehn agent grene was kilbot,&quot; but nothing ever happened. Maybe the tale adds some wonderful context that makes reading this worthwhile, but a mainlist article should be able to stand on its own. -1</p>
