<p>&quot;Killbot&quot; is a reference to <em>Futurama</em>, and was just me being a bit glib. Basically, it's a thing that kills people for no discernible reason<sup class="footnoteref"><a id="footnoteref-954254-1" href="javascript:;" class="footnoteref" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnote-954254-1')">1</a></sup>. The bit about the reveal was similarly me being glib. Basically, I expected there to be some sort of revelation at the end of the interview that would change the entire context for the piece.</p>
<p>I think that you're overestimating the power of visuals on an article. If you look at articles that have memorable pictures, such as 106 or 1867, you'll see that they compliment articles that already have fundamentally interesting ideas. Relying on a picture to carry an article that doesn't have much in the way of interesting concepts is not going to make the article interesting.</p>
<div class="footnotes-footer">
<div class="title">Footnotes</div>
<div class="footnote-footer" id="footnote-954254-1"><a href="javascript:;" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.utils.scrollToReference('footnoteref-954254-1')">1</a>. I get that it's an effect of it moving around and effectively suffocating people, but I don't really get the reasoning behind its creation. Basically, <em>how</em> it kills people is totally clear, but <em>why</em> it was created, which is more interesting, imo, is never explained.</div>
</div>

