			<div class="content" id="post-content-2404812">
									

<blockquote>
<p>An SCP article should work as a standalone document.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I think that unfortunately this attitude is very limiting to our creativity on this site. In-universe an SCP document would be only the very top page of a thick file of supporting documentation, yet it is nearly impossible to write an SCP that reveals itself through a series of documents because of the way that people vote on them as separate things. Many people have said that I should have written these things for <a href="/scp-2140">SCP-2140</a> but I have no doubt that a lengthy statistical study of people's biographies with 2140 clearance compared to controls would be downvoted into oblivion by the same people who are asking for it.</p>
<p>I had to create a fairly sophisticated work-around to make <a href="/scp-2111">SCP-2111</a> a single work rather than five separate ones. Really though, it is only this bizarre artifact of voting that required this.</p>
<p>Why <em>should</em> a story have to fit on one page to be considered a single work? We don't expect this of written fiction in print media. Author intent should count for something here. In this case these two peices are clearly connected as a larger work.</p>

