<p>-1</p>
<p>I got the :) image right away from the description. The &quot;You get a feeling when something is man-made,&quot; line, plus the shape, gave me the nudge immediately to realizing this was AWCY. Kudos for not having to say &quot;Are you cool yet??!!&quot; or some stupid thing in order to hammer that point in, but I still don't like it as a standalone article, for several reasons.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;whatever</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;whatever</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>I'm not a big fan of the hook here. There's the funny bit of it being a smiley face, and the slightly interesting story, but it's not enough for me. As others have said, maybe if we had some hints as to WHY or HOW this thing was created, it might be more interesting. But to me I find it a bit boring.</p>
<p>Additionally, I almost never like articles whose sole purpose is to tell a story in the retrieval log/interview log/exploration log. SCPs like that should be tales. This one even moreso, because it then links to a tale (which I haven't read yet), driving that point home like a punch in the gut.</p>
<p>I will respond to some one other comment here, so as not to clog the board.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>sirpudding:<br />
I think that unfortunately this attitude is very limiting to our creativity on this site. In-universe an SCP document would be only the very top page of a thick file of supporting documentation, yet it is nearly impossible to write an SCP that reveals itself through a series of documents because of the way that people vote on them as separate things. Many people have said that I should have written these things for SCP-2140 but I have no doubt that a lengthy statistical study of people's biographies with 2140 clearance compared to controls would be downvoted into oblivion by the same people who are asking for it.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That's the challenge isn't it? There are literally thousands of other creative writing sites on the internet, and there are also literally thousands of SCPs that stand very well on their own. If you don't like the format, you are free to write elsewhere, or write a tale, or add supplemental documents, or a bibliography, and see how well they are received. IMO, you never know which skip someone is going to randomly click on, and &quot;there is no canon,&quot; so every SCP does need to stand more or less on its own.</p>
<p>In my mind, the best skips have containment procedures, descriptions, interview logs/test logs/recover logs/letters and a myriad of other supplemental materials all working together in harmony to show an interesting picture. That can be a story, emotional or creepy or otherwise. That can be just an image of an interesting phenomenon. It can be a mystery to unravel, some kernel of truth the reader has to put together. I'm not saying this SCP doesn't try to do that; it does, but it tries and fails. It really seems like it was meant to be a tale.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

