<p>Thanks for your thoughts, Abettik. As with Gaffney, I'd be interested to hear further from you if you read the Tale as well. Do you think that it would be enough for you, that it would have a more interesting story overall, if presented as a single article? Or is your view that the whole thing should be a tale?</p>
<p>I agree with you that one of the interesting things about the site is the restriction imposed by the SCP format. But I'm not sure if I'm misinterpreting your comment:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I almost never like articles whose sole purpose is to tell a story in the retrieval log/interview log/exploration log.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>because I'm not sure where you draw the line. To take some famous/favourite examples, I would say that each of <a href="/scp-2188">SCP-2188</a> and <a href="/scp-093">SCP-093</a> tell stories through logs. Are those the type of articles you're referring to? What about <a href="/scp-087">SCP-087</a>? I think that also tells a story (albeit one that is very limited in range), but I'm not sure what side of the line it falls.</p>
<p>I'm not asking to be confrontational, quite the opposite. I am genuinely interested in how readers approach the site, and what they enjoy. It won't necessarily change how I write, but at least I'll understand when I'm pushing against the boundaries.</p>
<p>Edit: Also, I should comment on &quot;gave me the nudge immediately to realizing this was AWCY&quot;. If you read the Tale, you'll see that this is not quite how the anomaly was created. However it is true on a deeper level - part of the inspiration for this article was a post somewhere suggesting stories about GoIs interacting between themselves, and I thought &quot;Why doesn't MC&amp;D commission artwork from AWCY?&quot;.</p>

