<p>I've summarized responses to the above three posts herein.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Long&nbsp;response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Posting something labeled as a 'tale&quot; in SCP format also seems to attract downvotes for violating convention…</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I wouldn't recommend posting a &quot;tale&quot; in skip format. It could be done, but you could post a &quot;tale&quot; as a series of related documents, as psul successfully did in his accompanying tale, &quot;Figure and Ground.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>So I assume you think a fair good number of skips should be tales? Would you downvote them for that reason?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>When I come across one like that, yes, I think I do.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That sounds really restricting (almost elitist but I'm assuming you're not saying this with elitist intentions). We can't ever try something new or buck a format because we might as well go write it on some other site?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I'm not trying to sound elitist at all&#8230; and I am a sucker for <strong>good</strong> format-screws, so I don't mind people turning the format on its head. But the format is there for a reason. We're describing objects or places or phenomenon, and in doing so, telling a story. Not saying, &quot;here's an object, and here's a story to go with it&quot; which is what I consider &quot;Post-Incident Interview SCP-2162-A/Green/01&quot; to be. And the more I think about it, the more I think that's why I don't like it. It's not a recovery log, or an exploration log, it does almost nothing to tell us more about the skip itself. It's just there, as an added story. Now I did ready Figure and Ground, the accompaniment, and I liked it, and i think there's some stuff in there that DOES add some more to the skip, but this Incident log isn't it.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Thanks for your thoughts, Abettik. As with Gaffney, I'd be interested to hear further from you if you read the Tale as well. Do you think that it would be enough for you, that it would have a more interesting story overall, if presented as a single article? Or is your view that the whole thing should be a tale?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I did read it, and I did respond to it. The answers to these questions are there.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>To take some famous/favourite examples, I would say that each of SCP-2188 and SCP-093 tell stories through logs. Are those the type of articles you're referring to? What about SCP-087? I think that also tells a story (albeit one that is very limited in range), but I'm not sure what side of the line it falls.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have never read 2188, and I never could get through 093. I tried 093 and gave up. But for 087, those are exploration logs, and therefore, by definition, they are telling us more about the skip itself. I think your incident log here feels tacked on to me&#8230; it doesn't add anything to the skip itself. It doesn't tell us where it is or where it came from. It tells us why to fear it, but anyone with two brain cells would have known that already. It tells us how to detect it, but again, you already mention a detection method in your description.</p>
</div>
