			<div class="content" id="post-content-2406165">
									

<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;Show&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Hide&nbsp;Response</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>There is an SCP contained anomaly here. There are the related documents. You are downvoting the SCP because it isn't complete, but complaining that the related documents are too tale-like. The SCP couldn't be posted as part of the tale, because of the reason above. So what exactly do you expect here? It seems really &quot;damned if you, damned if you don't&quot;, to me.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I upvoted the &quot;related documents&quot; tale, and they are, well, a tale. I thought this anomaly works really well in that format and is a great example of how you can feature a new anomaly in a tale. Unlike the SCP itself, the &quot;related documents&quot; stand on their own (though I don't hold tales to that requirement anyways) and do not need a corresponding SCP.</p>
<p>What I'm saying is: &quot;if your skip doesn't work in SCP format, change the format and make it a tale.&quot; You seem to think I'm saying: &quot;if your skip doesn't work in SCP format, keep the SCP format but make it a tale.&quot; Nope.</p>
<p>Indeed, the &quot;Figure and Ground&quot; related documents are a GREAT example of a well-done tale written as a collection of several supporting documents that could potentially be featured in an SCP, but work better as a tale.</p>
<p>In my opinion, as it is currently written, the SCP does not stand on its own. Without the tale, the SCP falls flat. And the &quot;Incident Log&quot; used was probably the most boring part of the entire saga, and adds nothing to the SCP. The &quot;Incident Log&quot; is a superfluous story&#8230; and yet, when looking at the SCP on its own, the SCP seems to exist just to tell it.</p>
<p>I hold SCPs to a high standard. They are to be neat and clinical, paint an overall singular picture, with as much brevity and as little superfluous information as possible. I always read SCPs like I am a Level 4 researcher reading a summary of a much larger series of documents. Whatever assistant who created the skip brief has carefully chosen the most important documents to paint the best picture of the SCP as possible. In this case, I would ask that person, &quot;Why did you choose this incident log, and not the diary entries which are evidence towards who created it and why?&quot; I understand doing something from a narrative sense, but I believe that it falls flat in that aspect.</p>
<p>Anyways, I am done answering questions on the subject. I don't want this to look like an argument or anything. So if you or the author or anyone else have more questions, feel free to message me. :-)</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

